Generate config files from templates

Usage: evnx template [OPTIONS] --input <INPUT> --output <OUTPUT>

Options:
      --input <INPUT>
          The template to render. `{{VAR}}`, `${VAR}` and `$VAR` are substituted; filters are listed below

      --output <OUTPUT>
          Where to write the rendered file, or `-` for stdout.
          
          ⚠️ The result usually contains real values — see `--gitignore`. A new file is created readable by you only (0600). An existing file is only replaced with `--force`, and keeps its mode. The template and the env file are never overwritten.

      --force
          Replace an existing output file

      --follow-symlinks
          Write through an output that is a symlink to a file outside this folder. Without it, such an output is refused

      --mode <MODE>
          File mode for the output, in octal (e.g. 0640). Applied to a new or an existing file. Without it a new file is 0600

      --mkdir
          Create the output's missing directories, readable by you only (0700)

  -n, --dry-run
          Render and report what would be written, and write nothing.
          
          Fails where the real run would (an unknown filter, an unresolved placeholder without `--allow-unresolved`, a missing directory without `--mkdir`). Never prints the rendered text, and leaves `.gitignore` alone.

      --gitignore
          Automatically add the output file to .gitignore (no prompt). Useful in CI scripts. Mutually exclusive with --no-gitignore

      --no-gitignore
          Skip all .gitignore checks and warnings. Use when you manage .gitignore externally. Mutually exclusive with --gitignore

      --allow-unresolved
          Write an unresolved placeholder through literally and exit 0.
          
          Since 0.11.0 a placeholder with no value is an error: nothing is written, every unresolved name is listed, and the exit code is 2. An unresolved `{{DATABASE_URL}}` in a generated config is read by whatever runs next as the literal setting. Give a variable a fallback with `{{NAME|default:value}}` to keep it optional.

      --strict
          The default since 0.11.0; accepted, and removed in 1.0

  -v, --verbose
          Enable verbose output

  -q, --quiet
          Print nothing on success. Findings, warnings, errors and requested data still print

      --no-color
          Disable colored output

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

  -V, --version
          Print version

Files:
      --env <FILE>
          The environment file to take values from. Defaults to `.env`, or `[defaults] env_name` from `.evnx.toml`

      --env-name <NAME>
          Use `.env.<NAME>` instead of `.env`

Confirmation:
  -y, --yes
          Do not ask: add the output file to .gitignore without the prompt (as `--gitignore`). An explicit `--no-gitignore` wins.

References:
  {{VAR}}  ${VAR}  $VAR        replaced by the value from the env file

Filters: {{VAR|filter}}, chained left to right: {{VAR|default:8080|int}}
  upper  lower                 case
  title                        Title Case For Each Word
  capitalize                   First character only
  bool                         true/yes/on/1/y or false/no/off/0/n; anything else is an error
  int                          a whole number; anything else is an error
  json  yaml  toml            a quoted, escaped string for that syntax
  sh                           one shell word, single-quoted
  default:VALUE                VALUE when VAR is empty or not defined
An unknown filter is an error and nothing is written.
/${VAR} /{{VAR}} /$VAR are written as they are, without the backslash.
$VAR is replaced only when VAR is defined, and never reported.
A placeholder with no value is an error too (exit 2); --allow-unresolved
writes it through as it is.

Full guide: https://docs.evnx.dev/cli/commands/template
